# Allowed sites of each structure pattern, per file. A line is `ROW KIND COUNT FILE`; KIND is
# `prod`, or `test` for tests/ directories, tests.rs and *_tests.rs, and, for disallowed_methods, an
# expect under #[cfg(test)] on an item, statement or expression, or in #[cfg_attr(test, ..)]. structure-check fails when a
# file has more sites than its line allows (none without a line), and when it has fewer: lower the
# line to the new count and delete it at 0. Counts only fall: `check --changed` also fails when a
# count is above the one the allowlist at the base revision holds, so a rise is the lead's act on main.
# Under `--against`, a file's test count may rise by as much as the same row's prod count in that file
# fell.
# Every row scans crates/.
#
# disallowed_methods: mentions of clippy::disallowed_methods in the lint list of an expect, allow,
#                     warn, deny or forbid attribute, and in the tokens of a macro. The
#                     lint rejects storage::page::owner, pages::Exclusive::write,
#                     PageMemory::lock_exclusive, PageMemory::try_lock_exclusive and
#                     Optimistic::upgrade (clippy.toml); each allowed site carries an expect, or its
#                     file one inner expect where the file owns the item.
# database_marks:     `Quarantine::Database`, `Mark::Database` and `Mark::Held` in production sources
#                     of crates/ outside storage/policy.rs: what a fault marks is decided by the
#                     policy table. Not counted: the marks page's codec, and reads of the marks
#                     (`is_quarantined(..)`, `any_of` of an object with the database,
#                     `quarantined_name(..)`, the admission loop's `std::iter::once(..)`, and the
#                     failure naming the database once it is marked).
# mark_setters:       calls of `.quarantine(`, `marks().hold(` and `marks().change(` in production
#                     sources of crates/: a mark is set by the mapper, which takes a witness of commit
#                     order. The lines left are the mapper and the pager's setters.
# index_marks:        `Quarantine::Index`, and an import of it (`Quarantine::*`, `Quarantine::{.. Index
#                     ..}`), in production sources of crates/ outside storage/policy.rs and
#                     storage/quarantine.rs (the marks page's codec): a finding marks its index by the
#                     policy table. The line left is catalog/structures.rs, the one builder of an
#                     index's holder. Not counted: reads of the marks (`is_quarantined(..)`, a comparison, `contains(&..)`,
#                     the admission checks' `marked(..)` and `(id, _)|` mapping, `matches!(m, ..)`),
#                     the mark a reindex releases, and the testing oracle's `Ok(..)`.
# marked_readers:     in production sources of crates/ outside storage/ and mvcc/, calls of
#                     `is_quarantined` whose argument is `Quarantine::Database`, spelled bare or as
#                     `storage::` or `crate::storage::Quarantine::Database`, across lines too, and
#                     calls of `admit_database(` (not its definition), and
#                     `std::iter::once(Quarantine::Database)` in the same spellings, the
#                     admission loop over the marks: reads of the database's mark
#                     alone. Whether the database is refused whole is `Pager::refused_whole`. The
#                     lines left ask whether the database is marked: the write path's admission and
#                     its callers, its rewrite of a failure of the transactions layer, and the
#                     damage a failed open answers `verify` over.
# api_aborts:         calls of `.abort(` in production sources of crates/adtb/src/api: an abort whose
#                     failure the caller returns. A transaction ended after a failure goes through
#                     `abandon`, which decides the call's reply. The lines left end a transaction
#                     with no failure: a rollback, a drop, and a write rerun after a wait.
# crash_loops:        definitions of a function whose name holds `crash_loop`: the harness's one
#                     crash loop, which runs the ledger check after every round.
# crash_allows:       definitions of `fn allows`: what a crash loop's reopened database tolerates.
# crash_loops and crash_allows count names: a helper under another name escapes them.
